---
layout: article
title: Metrics
description: How Appwrite Analytics calculates visitors, visits, pageviews, bounce rate, visit duration, and engagement time, which breakdowns it offers, and how to read metrics from your own code.
---

Every figure on an analytics dashboard comes from the events a property receives. This page defines each metric and breakdown, and shows how to read the same numbers from code.

# Dashboard metrics {% #dashboard-metrics %}

{% only_dark %}
![Dashboard with the Bounce rate tab selected and the bounce rate plotted on the chart](/images/docs/analytics/dark/metrics.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Dashboard with the Bounce rate tab selected and the bounce rate plotted on the chart](/images/docs/analytics/metrics.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The row of metrics at the top of the dashboard covers the selected date range.

- **Unique visitors**: distinct visitors in the range. Visitor IDs change every day, so someone who returns on three days counts three times. See [Visitors and sessions](/docs/products/analytics/visitors).
- **Visits**: visits that started in the range. A visit that started earlier doesn't count, even if it continued into the range.
- **Pageviews**: `pageview` and `screen_view` events, including repeat views of the same page or screen.
- **Events**: every event in the range, automatic and custom.
- **Users**: distinct signed-in users, from events that carry a user ID. Anonymous visitors aren't counted.
- **Views per visit**: pageviews divided by visits.
- **Bounce rate**: the share of visits with fewer than two views, no interaction, and less than 10 seconds between their first and last event.
- **Visit duration**: the average time from a visit's first event to its last. A visit with a single event counts as 0 seconds.
- **Engagement time**: the average active time per visit, counting only visits that reported any. On the web, a page is active while it's visible. In a mobile app, the app is active while it's in the foreground.

**Bounce rate** and **Visit duration** cover the same visits as **Visits**: the ones that started in the range. The **Visits** chart is different: each point counts the visits with an event in that hour or day, so a visit that crosses midnight appears on both days, and the points can add up to more than the total.

With a **Page**, **Event**, **User**, or prop filter (a value from **Event properties**), the dashboard can't calculate totals for the whole range, so **Unique visitors** and **Visits** add up the chart's points. With **1h**, a visitor active in several hours then counts once for each hour, and a visit that spans two points counts in both.

Exports and the API also include **Scroll depth**, the average of the deepest scroll positions that pages reported, from `0` to `100` percent. The Web SDK reports a page's scroll depth only if the visitor scrolled it.

Without a filter, these metrics include bot traffic. The **Humans vs bots** card shows how much of it comes from bots, and **Mobile** properties open with a **Humans only** filter. For how Appwrite identifies visitors and splits their events into visits, see [Visitors and sessions](/docs/products/analytics/visitors).

## Mobile and Agent properties {% #property-types %}

{% only_dark %}
![Dashboard of a Mobile property with Active users, Sessions, Screen views, Signed-in users, Screens/session, and Session length](/images/docs/analytics/dark/metrics-mobile.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Dashboard of a Mobile property with Active users, Sessions, Screen views, Signed-in users, Screens/session, and Session length](/images/docs/analytics/metrics-mobile.avif)
{% /only_light %}

**Mobile** properties use app terms: **Active users** for **Unique visitors**, **Sessions** for **Visits**, **Screen views** for **Pageviews**, **Signed-in users** for **Users**, **Screens/session** for **Views per visit**, and **Session length** for **Visit duration**. They have no bounce rate. **Agent** properties show **Events**, **Visits**, **Users**, and **Visit duration**.

# Breakdowns {% #breakdowns %}

{% only_dark %}
![Technology card on the Operating systems tab, next to the AI agents and crawlers card](/images/docs/analytics/dark/breakdowns.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Technology card on the Operating systems tab, next to the AI agents and crawlers card](/images/docs/analytics/breakdowns.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The cards below the chart break traffic down by one dimension at a time and rank the values by visitors. **Custom events** and **Models and tools** rank by how many times each event or call was sent, and **Users** ranks users by visits. The API uses the same dimensions, by the names in this table, and ranks rows by visitors, except `eventName`, which it ranks by event count. Unlike the card, `eventName` also includes the automatic events.

| Dimension | Dashboard card | Values |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `channel` | Traffic sources | `direct`, `search`, `ai`, `social`, `email`, `paid`, or `referral` |
| `referrerSource` | Traffic sources | The referring hostname, such as `google.com` |
| `utmSource`, `utmMedium`, `utmCampaign`, `utmContent`, `utmTerm` | Traffic sources | UTM parameters from the URL a visitor landed on |
| `page` | Pages, or Screens on mobile | The page path, such as `/pricing` |
| `entryPage`, `exitPage` | Pages, or Screens on mobile | The first and last page of each visit |
| `hostname` | Pages, or Technology's Platforms tab on mobile | The hostname of the page, or the app's platform, such as `android` |
| `country` | Locations | Two-letter country code, such as `US`. The dashboard shows country names. |
| `region`, `city` | Locations | Region and city names, such as `California` |
| `browser` | Technology | Such as `Chrome`, `Mobile Safari`, or `Firefox` |
| `operatingSystem` | Technology | Such as `Windows`, `iOS`, `Mac`, or `Android` |
| `device` | Technology | Such as `desktop`, `smartphone`, or `tablet` |
| `screenSize` | Technology | `mobile`, `tablet`, `laptop`, or `desktop` |
| `browserVersion`, `operatingSystemVersion` | Technology's Versions tabs | Such as `147.0` and `18.5` |
| `trafficType` | Humans vs bots | `human` or `bot` |
| `botCategory`, `botName` | Humans vs bots, AI agents and crawlers | Such as `ai crawler` and `GPTBot` |
| `eventName` | Custom events | The event name |
| `userId` | Users | The user ID of signed-in traffic. Anonymous events are left out. |
| `props` | Event properties | The prop keys, or with `propKey`, the values of one key |

Each row has the number of visitors, visits, events, and signed-in users with that value. On **Entry pages** and **Exit pages**, the API returns `users` as `null`. In the API, a breakdown's visits are the `sessions` field: the visits that started in the range on **Entry pages** and **Exit pages**, and the visits with an event in the range on every other breakdown. For how sources and channels are worked out, see [Traffic sources](/docs/products/analytics/sources). For bot categories, see [Bots and AI traffic](/docs/products/analytics/bots).

Screen sizes come from the viewport width: `mobile` below 576 pixels, `tablet` from 576 to 991, `laptop` from 992 to 1439, and `desktop` from 1440 up.

# Read metrics from your code {% #api %}

Read metrics with `listMetrics` and an API key that has the `analytics.read` scope. To create one, follow the steps in [Track events from your server](/docs/products/analytics/server-side#create-an-api-key) and select `analytics.read` instead.

This example lists the five countries with the most visitors over the last seven days.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
import { Client, Analytics, AnalyticsDimension } from 'node-appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const analytics = new Analytics(client);

const countries = await analytics.listMetrics({
    propertyId: '<PROPERTY_ID>',
    dimensions: [AnalyticsDimension.Country],
    dateRange: '7d',
    limit: 5,
});

for (const row of countries.metrics) {
    console.log(row.value, row.visitors);
}
```
```server-deno
